{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794760867620,"sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd9ffeEQ4EaGVDFSUKgQ","part":{"partID":"prt_1e0157dcc002ZFDB4RpvnAWFTu","sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd9ffeEQ4EaGVDFSUKgQ","messageID":"msg_1e0156878001ZZaBkDSiCFVBwb","type":"tool","id":"call_a3abeb979f0e4f5387a79f75","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md"},"output":"Read file .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md, lines 1-219\n1: ---\n2: title: \"system skill advisor runtime scripts routing accuracy: Code README\"\n3: description: \"Code-facing README for .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"system-skill-advisor runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy\"\n6:   - \"code README\"\n7: ---\n8: \n9: # system skill advisor runtime scripts routing accuracy\n10: \n11: Operator and maintenance scripts for this skill.\n12: \n13: ---\n14: \n15: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n16: \n17: ### Purpose\n18: \n19: This README documents the code-bearing folder `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy` so operators can understand its role without opening every source file first. It follows the sk-doc skill README structure while staying focused on code navigation.\n20: \n21: ### Usage\n22: \n23: Use this file to identify the folder boundary, the likely verification path and the local source files that need sk-code conventions. Keep behavior details in source comments and higher-level workflow details in the owning `SKILL.md`.\n24: \n25: ### Key Statistics\n26: \n27: | Metric | Value |\n28: |---|---:|\n29: | Code files | 14 |\n30: | README scope | Direct files in this folder |\n31: | Audit context | Internal validation notes |\n32: \n33: ---\n34: \n35: ## 2. QUICK START\n36: \n37: **Step 1: Confirm the owner.**\n38: \n39: Start with `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/SKILL.md` for runtime routing and workflow boundaries.\n40: \n41: **Step 2: Inspect the local code.**\n42: \n43: ```bash\n44: rg --files .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy\n45: ```\n46: \n47: Expected result: the command lists the source files summarized below.\n48: \n49: **Step 3: Verify changes.**\n50: \n51: Run individual scripts from the repository root with the documented arguments.\n52: \n53: ---\n54: \n55: ## 3. FEATURES\n56: \n57: | Feature | What It Does |\n58: |---|---|\n59: | Folder boundary | Documents direct code files under `runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy`. |\n60: | sk-code alignment | Points reviewers at OpenCode naming, header, error-handling and type-discipline checks. |\n61: | Verification handoff | Records the expected owner and audit packet for follow-up work. |\n62: \n63: ### Attribution on every run line\n64: \n65: Each line a behaviour run appends carries four fields beside the locator, exit and changed paths:\n66: \n67: - `offeredCommand` is the slash command the advisor head named, or `null` when the head named none.\n68: - `offeredPolicy` is the policy that directly follows that command, one of `run`, `start` or `ask`, or `null` when no policy follows it.\n69: - `offeredAs` says where that command sat in the head: `head`, `partner` when it followed `vs` on an ambiguous head, or `null` when the head named none.\n70: - `briefDelivered` says whether the advisor brief reached the session. It is `true` or `false` where the runtime keeps a readable record, and `unknown` where none exists or cannot be read, so a gap in the record never reads as a miss. A line written when the head moved stops before the offer is read, so it carries none of the four and holds headMoved instead.\n71: \n72: The offer is a replay of the Claude prompt hook after the turn instead of a copy of the live session head. The head is the only text that names both the command and the policy, and not every runtime keeps it, so `replayOffer` runs the checkout's own hook and records what that checkout would have offered at the time of the run. A replay that finds no hook, a failing hook or a head without an offer writes null fields plus an `offerError` of `hook_missing`, `hook_failed` or `no_head`, so one bad line never aborts a run.\n73: \n74: ### Backfill a finished log\n75: \n76: `run-command-behaviour.mjs --backfill <run-log> --out <new-log>` adds those four fields to a log written before they existed, and a `--backfill` run without `--out` is a usage error that exits 2. The mode refuses with exit 2 and a JSON body holding its `code`:\n77: \n78: - `same_path` when `--out` resolves to the input log, because the backfill writes a new file.\n79: - `holdout_row` or `unknown_row` when the corpus refuses a row id, because every prompt is resolved before the first line is written and a refused row leaves no output file behind.\n80: \n81: A line whose locator names no checkout keeps null offer fields and `offerError` `no_checkout`, and the rest of the log is still written with `offerBackfilled: true`.\n82: \n83: A line whose checkout is neither this project root nor an arena holding the `skilled-behaviour-arena` marker also keeps null offer fields, with `offerError` `untrusted_checkout`, because a run log is input and the folder it names may hold any code. Nothing from that folder runs, and the rest of the log is still written.\n84: \n85: `--brief-only` keeps every offer field exactly as the input line holds it, present or absent, and recomputes only `briefDelivered`, because a recorded offer belongs to the head the turn saw and replaying it would describe a different head. Brief delivery now comes from the session records of Pi, Devin, DSH and Codex, while OpenCode stays `unknown` because its plugin adds the brief in memory and the store keeps no copy.\n86: \n87: ### The typed attachment block\n88: \n89: `capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs` reports typed reach twice under `attachedMetrics`:\n90: \n91: - `attachedMetrics.typed` counts `own`, `attached`, `combined` and `total` across the typed rows. `own` is a row the ranked bridge already landed, `attached` is a row the typed command itself delivered, and `combined` is their sum.\n92: - `attachedMetrics.typedRows` keys one record per typed row id in sorted order with `expected_command`, `top`, `carried`, `command` and `policy`. `carried` is `own`, `attached` or null, so a missed row names the command it did carry instead of only lowering a count.\n93: \n94: A typed command travels on its owner. It is delivered only when the leading recommendation is that command's owner skill or carries the same command row, so an unrelated leading skill never presents it. A typed command with no scorer row of its own has no other path to the reader, so its owner is ranked first.\n95: \n96: ### Measure the brief cost\n97: \n98: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/measure-brief-cost.mjs` reports what the Claude prompt hook actually sends the model for a typed row.\n99: \n100: - Flags: `node measure-brief-cost.mjs [--rows <id,id>] [--root <dir>]` keeps every typed row on the `none` split, `--rows` narrows the run to named corpus ids and `--root` names the checkout the corpus and hook are read from, defaulting to the current directory. A holdout or unknown id exits 2 before anything is spawned, and no prompt is ever printed.\n101: - Output keys: one JSON object holds `rows`, then `liveRows`, `totalBytes`, `medianBytes`, `maxBytes`, `medianHeadBytes` and `maxHeadBytes`. Each entry of `rows` carries `id`, `bytes`, `headBytes`, `estimatedTokens` and `live`, where bytes are the UTF-8 length of `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`, headBytes is the UTF-8 length of its first line, live says whether that first line starts `Advisor: live`, and tokens divide the context bytes by four, the renderer's own characters per token. Every byte statistic is taken over live rows only.\n102: - Refusal: a run with no live head prints `{\"error\":\"degraded_brief\",...}` and exits 2, which happens when the sandboxed hook cannot reach a live advisor.\n103: - Sandbox: the run makes one `ssa-cost-` directory under the OS temp dir and sets `SPECKIT_IPC_SOCKET_DIR` to it for every hook process, so the operator's warm daemon is never reached. Each hook process also runs without `AI_SESSION_CHILD`, so a run from a dispatched worker measures the brief an operator's own session receives rather than the child form. Each hook child runs with `TMPDIR` set to the sandbox, so a cost run writes no health record into the shared metrics folder. At the end it stops only a process whose command line names that directory and removes it.\n104: \n105: ### Two arms on one commit\n106: \n107: `run-command-behaviour.mjs --arm off` and `--arm on` measure one commit twice. A run under either arm starts from an environment where the advisor switch comes from the arm and not from the operator's shell, so the turn environment differs only in the advisor switch. `--run <n>` takes a whole number from 1 and records it on every line of that run, so two samples of one row stay apart and a retried line still replaces the first line of its own run. After each turn the runner also puts back a `CLAUDE.md` that the turn changed and git does not track, and marks that line `pointerRestored`.\n108: \n109: `--set fresh` reads `behaviour-fresh-prompts.jsonl` beside the script, a file the capture never publishes. The set refuses an absent file with `fresh_missing`, a row whose split is not `fresh` with `fresh_split`, an id that does not start `fresh-` with `fresh_id`, a file that does not hold 36 rows with `set_short`, and a prompt that repeats another row once case and whitespace are folded with `repeated_row`. A fresh row runs only under arm `off` or `on` (`fresh_arm`) and only with `--run` (`fresh_run`).\n110: \n111: `prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs` leaves a `CLAUDE.md` holding `@AGENTS.md` in an arena that has none and appends `/CLAUDE.md` to `.git/info/exclude`, so Claude Code imports the arena's own rules while the clean-tree check and the head stay as they were.\n112: \n113: A summary pairs the two arms row by row. `--pair off,on` gives a runtime a `paired` block when both arms ran and either one scored an owned row, and a row whose arms scored a different number of runs, including a row one arm never scored, adds to `unpaired` and to no other count. `pooled` sums those blocks over every runtime and sets `p` to the exact one-sided sign test over `gained` and `lost`, rounded to four decimals and null when no pair moved either way. A reader that walks every top-level key of the summary must skip `pooled`, because it is not a runtime.\n114: \n115: Each arm prints `declineStarts` and `skillOnlyStarts` as `x of y`, plus one line per entry of `commandStartRuns`. A decline or skill-only run started a tracked command when a skill call named it, a `COMMAND:` line reported it `started`, the host expanded it, or the run read the command's document and also wrote inside its checkout. A read with no write is a lookup and not a start, and a search, an in-place `sed` or a heredoc line is not a read.\n116: \n117: ### Reading the outcome\n118: \n119: - A loaded document is not a followed step, so `loadRate` and `followedRange` say a workflow opened, not that its steps were taken.\n120: - The behaviour rows are tuned rows, so `followedOffered` and `followedNotOffered` describe the corpus the brief was built against, not a fresh one.\n121: - Each row ran once, so one run moves a rate by more than the range around it can settle.\n122: - Two arms differ in more than the brief, so a `--pair` gain or loss is not the brief's effect alone.\n123: - The offer on a stored run of a runtime other than Claude is the Claude hook's replay, recorded when the run was made, so the split by offer says what the Claude head carried for that row.\n124: \n125: ---\n126: \n127: ## 4. STRUCTURE\n128: \n129: | Path | Purpose |\n130: |---|---|\n131: | `gate3-corpus-runner.mjs` | MJS source file in this folder. |\n132: | `score-routing-corpus.py` | PY source file in this folder. |\n133: | `command-prompts.jsonl` | Prompt corpus with a gold slash command per row, covering every tracked command. |\n134: | `capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs` | Scores `command-prompts.jsonl` under the pinned regime and prints or writes the baseline. Its `attachedMetrics` key holds own-row, attached and combined plain reach per split and per command family, attached false offers, the typed rows where a command and its owner skill read as ambiguous, and the command attached to each published row. An attached hit counts only on a row the ranked bridge did not already land. Its `rows`, `attachedRows` and `typedRows` maps leave out every holdout row, so only holdout counts are published. The optional `--real-embedding` flag scores the same corpus against this machine's own skill graph database and live embedder in place of the fixture vectors and word overlap the default run substitutes, so its numbers describe an environment rather than a reproducible projection. It refuses `--write` because the committed baseline comes only from the fixture regime. Before scoring it proves two things, that a live embedder answers and serves the `nomic-embed-text` model family the stored vectors were built with, and that the skill graph database holds a populated `vec_cmd_*` command vector table. A missing piece exits 2 with a message that names it. Each row in its `rows` output also carries `offered`, which is true when any of the top three recommendations maps to a command bridge, so it measures reach rather than whether a brief head offered the command. Its `attachedMetrics.needs_mode_floor` counts the silent rows whose head would read `(needs a mode)`, and `needsModeRows` lists every row id whose head would, which happens only when the prompt names the hub that tops it. `attachedMetrics.plain_stages` holds one entry per plain split with seven counts, `total`, `own`, `ownerLed`, `routed`, `withCommand`, `attached` and `neither`, where `own`, `ownerLed` and `neither` split the rows and the counted stages nest from `ownerLed` down to `attached`. `attachedMetrics.fit` holds the two fit classes, `skill... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n135: | `score-command-transcripts.mjs` | Scores captured sessions through the shared session readers. `--summary <run-log> [<run-log>...]` scores every row one or more run logs list, per runtime and arm, and `--runtime <name> --locator '<json>'` scores the one session a locator names. Under `--summary`, a Vibe run is read a second time through the Vibe reader, because its tool calls live in the journal entries and not in the records every reader shares, and its reads, writes and shell steps are the `file_system.read_file`, `file_system.write_file`, `file_system.edit` and `file_system.bash` calls its journal records. `--row-id <id>` narrows `--summary` to one row and is a usage error with `--runtime`. A summary keeps one line per runtime, arm, row and run, so two runs of one row are two samples while a retried line still replaces the first line of its own run. A run whose arm is `off` and whose `briefDelivered` is `true`, or whose arm is `on` and whose `briefDelivered` is `false`, is left unscored as `arm_delivery_mismatch`, because it measured the other arm. `--json` prints machine-readable output. `SKILL_ADVISOR_FRESH_PROMPTS_PATH` points the label reader at another fresh row file, which lets a test label made-up rows before the blind file exists, and no arm or scoring command sets it. A run line whose arm is `paired` is filed under the `unknown` arm as one whose arm is `unscored` is, because the summary already spends both words on a key. `--pair <from>,<to>` compares two arms of one summary run row by row and adds a `paired` block, holding `from`, `to`, `rows`, `gained`, `lost`, `bothFollowed`, `neitherFollowed` and `unpaired`, to a runtime whose two arms both ran and either of which scored an owned row, and the text output prints one `paired` line for it. `--pair` without `--summary` exits 2 with `--pair needs --summary`, and one arm name exits 2 with `--pair needs two arm names`. A row whose arms scored a different number of runs, including a row one arm never score... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n136: | `command-offer.mjs` | Recovers the attribution fields for a run line. `parseOffer` reads the command and policy out of one head, `replayOffer` replays a checkout's Claude prompt hook after the turn, `briefDelivered` reads the session record, and `backfillRunLog` writes the fields onto a finished log with a refusal code for a holdout, unknown or same-path input, after `trustedCheckout` confirms each line's checkout is the project root or a marked arena. Each offer carries `offeredAs`, `head` or `partner`. A fallback head such as `Advisor: no skill matched.` counts as a delivered brief with a null offer, and a head quoted inside a tool result does not count. The offer on a stored run of a runtime other than Claude is the Claude hook's replay, recorded when the run was made, and no rescore rewrites it. |\n137: | `run-command-behaviour.mjs` | Runs one live turn per row and records the locator, exit and changed paths. `--print-argv <runtime>` prints a runtime's argv, `--list <set>` prints a set's row ids, and a run takes `--runtime` with `--set` or repeated `--row-id`, `--out <run-log>`, optional `--arm`, `--fixture` and `--dry-run`. Without `--arm`, a selection whose ids are all started-command rows runs under arm `g2`, one that holds none runs under `pre` and a mix is refused with `mixed_row_arms`. The `off` and `on` arms compare one commit with the advisor switched off and on, where a turn under either drops every inherited name that ends `_DISABLED` and holds `SKILL_ADVISOR` or `HOOKS`, and the `off` arm then sets `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED` and `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED` to `1`. `--run <n>` takes a whole number from 1 and records it on every line of that run, so two samples of one row stay apart. The `fresh` set reads `behaviour-fresh-prompts.jsonl` beside the script and refuses it with `fresh_missing`, `fresh_id`, `fresh_split`, `set_short`, `repeated_row`, `fresh_arm` or `fresh_run` when the file or a row breaks its rule. Each `g2` run line records the `fixture` folder its prompt named, and `--backfill` replays that folder, or `g2-fixture` beside the log for a line that holds none. `--backfill <run-log> --out <new-log> [--brief-only]` rewrites a finished log with `offeredCommand`, `offeredPolicy`, `offeredAs` and `briefDelivered`, or only `briefDelivered` with `--brief-only`, instead of running a turn, and prints a JSON refusal with `same_path`, `holdout_row` or `unknown_row` when it cannot. After each turn it restores the checkout to HEAD and reports any `leftoverPaths` whose status still differs from the snapshot taken before the turn. Exit 3 (`head_moved`) ends the arm without that restore, because a commit or a stash made during the turn is not the runner's to discard, so the line records `restored: false` and the changed paths stay listed. A... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n138: | `command-routing-baseline.json` | Captured command routing numbers that `tests/command-routing-slice.vitest.ts` holds the scorer to. |\n139: | `build-holdout.mjs` | Builds the frozen holdout from three separately authored fixtures that are disjoint from the labeled corpus, copying each row's own label and keeping its provenance in `source_type` and `origin_fixture`. Family-normalized dedup keeps the first occurrence and drops any prompt that collides with the training corpus. `--write` rewrites `holdout-prompts.jsonl`. |\n140: | `capture-local-native-divergence-ledger.mjs` | Recomputes the strict Python/native top-1 divergence set the ratchet replays. An existing approval is kept only while its prompt hash and both current tops still match, so the output is a capture of live scoring rather than a hand-edited fixture. Scores both scorers against a skill graph indexed fresh into a temporary directory with the clock pinned to the capture day, and `--write` records the result. |\n141: | `capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs` | Captures scorer accuracy across the full corpus, the independent holdout, the frozen ambiguity slice and the named intent buckets under the reproducible filesystem projection, then `--write` records `scorer-eval-baseline.json`. Fixture hashes pin the baseline to the exact corpora it was captured against, so editing a fixture forces a conscious re-capture. `--misses` prints one failing prompt per line. |\n142: | `derive-ambiguity-slice.mjs` | Derives the frozen ambiguity slice as the corpus rows whose top-2 fused-score margin is at or below `tau`, keeping each row's existing gold label, so a regression that only hurts contested prompts still fails a gate. `--write` rewrites `ambiguity-prompts.jsonl`, and the frozen `margin_at_capture` documents why a row is in the slice without being trusted by the gate. |\n143: | `prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs` | Builds the arena a live arm runs in: a clone of the branch tip with no remote and no outer repository, plus the ignored build trees copied in and stamped at copy time so the freshness guard reads them as fresh. It refuses a destination inside a repository or one that already exists, and skips every file whose name begins with `.env` so no secret follows the build output in. When the clone holds no `CLAUDE.md`, the builder writes one holding `@AGENTS.md` and appends `/CLAUDE.md` to `.git/info/exclude`, so Claude Code imports the arena's own rules while the clean-tree check and the head stay as they were. |\n144: | `reject-unknown-options.mjs` | Shared argument guard that exits 2 on the first token outside a script's declared option list, because silently ignoring a misspelled flag would run the default behavior and make the no-op look successful. |\n145: | `score-outcome-rerank.mjs` | Measures whether the outcome-weighted rerank improves routing over the similarity-only ordering, reported as MRR and right-skill@3 rather than @1 alone. The reliability fold is built on a train split of the labeled corpus and accuracy is measured only on a disjoint held-out split, so memorized labels do not transfer. Read-only against the skill graph. |\n146: | `worktree-restore.mjs` | Restores one measured turn's checkout to the snapshot taken before it, leaving a path that was already dirty alone and dropping a staged path HEAD does not know. A second snapshot after the restore reports what still differs, so the caller fails the run instead of claiming a clean worktree. |\n147: | `ambiguity-prompts.jsonl` | Frozen ambiguity slice written by `derive-ambiguity-slice.mjs` and read by `capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs`. Each row carries `id`, `prompt`, `skill_top_1`, `bucket`, `source_type`, `tau`, `margin_at_capture` and `captured_at`. |\n148: | `doctor-prompts.jsonl` | Doctor routing slice read by `tests/doctor-routing-slice.vitest.ts`. Each row carries `id`, `prompt`, `expected_top`, `expected_second` and `wave`, where a wave scores only after every doctor bridge its rows expect is active, so a half-shipped bridge cannot fail the gate. |\n149: | `holdout-prompts.jsonl` | Frozen holdout first written by `build-holdout.mjs` from three separately authored fixtures disjoint from the labeled corpus, and read by `capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs` as its unseen split. The file has been edited by hand since it was sealed, so `build-holdout.mjs --write` would now write a different set of rows. A sealed row is retired by deleting its line and recapturing `scorer-eval-baseline.json`, whose `holdoutSha256` fails the ratchet until that recapture runs. |\n150: | `labeled-prompts.jsonl` | Labeled training corpus with `id`, `prompt`, `skill_top_1`, `skill_correct`, `bucket`, `source_type`, `notes`, `gate3_triggers` and `gate3_reason_category`. Scored by `score-routing-corpus.py`, `score-outcome-rerank.mjs` and `capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs`, and read by the divergence ledger, the ambiguity deriver, the holdout builder and the gate 3 corpus runner. |\n151: | `scorer-eval-baseline.json` | Scorer eval baseline written by `capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs`: `schemaVersion`, `capturedAt`, `capturedAtSha`, `env`, the fixture hashes and `metrics` for the full corpus, holdout, ambiguity slice, gold-none false fire, intent buckets and unknown count. The ratchet gate re-scores live under the same environment and holds every metric to it. |\n152: \n153: ---\n154: \n155: ## 5. CONFIGURATION\n156: \n157: | Setting | Default | Purpose |\n158: |---|---|---|\n159: | sk-code surface | OPENCODE | Applies OpenCode TypeScript, JavaScript, Python, Shell and config conventions. |\n160: | README scope | Direct folder | This file documents this folder, not sibling folders. |\n161: \n162: ---\n163: \n164: ## 6. USAGE EXAMPLES\n165: \n166: **Audit this folder**\n167: \n168: ```text\n169: User request: Check .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy for sk-code and README coverage.\n170: Skill routing: sk-code plus sk-doc.\n171: Expected output: Findings recorded in the 026 audit report.\n172: ```\n173: \n174: **Score captured runs**\n175: \n176: ```bash\n177: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs \\\n178:   --summary scratch/runs-pre.jsonl scratch/runs-post.jsonl --json\n179: ```\n180: \n181: Exit 2 means no score was produced. A missing or malformed log prints `run_log_missing` or `run_log_malformed`, and a session the reader cannot open prints its own code, such as `capability_gated`.\n182: \n183: **Inspect and run one behaviour turn**\n184: \n185: ```bash\n186: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs --print-argv claude\n187: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs \\\n188:   --runtime claude --set chat --out scratch/runs-pre.jsonl --dry-run\n189: ```\n190: \n191: Exit 2 means the run was refused before or during its first prompt: bad flags, an unknown runtime or set, a holdout row, a dirty worktree outside the run log's folder, or a checkout that still has a git remote.\n192: \n193: **Backfill a finished log**\n194: \n195: ```bash\n196: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs \\\n197:   --backfill scratch/runs-pre.jsonl --out scratch/runs-pre-attributed.jsonl\n198: ```\n199: \n200: The output log holds the same lines plus `offeredCommand`, `offeredPolicy`, `offeredAs`, `offerError`, `briefDelivered` and `offerBackfilled`. Exit 2 means the output was refused, and the printed JSON names why: `same_path`, `holdout_row`, `unknown_row` or a missing `--out`.\n201: \n202: ---\n203: \n204: ## 7. TROUBLESHOOTING\n205: \n206: | What You See | Cause | Fix |\n207: |---|---|---|\n208: | README appears stale | Source files changed after this audit | Refresh the structure table and rerun the 026 audit check. |\n209: | Verification command is unclear | Folder is a helper boundary | Use the nearest package or skill-level verification command. |\n210: \n211: ---\n212: \n213: ## 8. RELATED DOCUMENTS\n214: \n215: | Document | Purpose |\n216: |---|---|\n217: | [`system-skill-advisor/SKILL.md`](../../../SKILL.md) | Runtime instructions for the owning skill. |\n218: | [`sk-code/SKILL.md`](../../../../sk-code/SKILL.md) | OpenCode coding standards and verification routing. |\n219: | [`sk-doc skill-readme-template.md`](../../../../sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-readme-template.md) | README structure used for this code README. |","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md, lines 1-219\n1: ---\n2: title: \"system skill advisor runtime scripts routing accuracy: Code README\"\n3: description: \"Code-facing README for .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"system-skill-advisor runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy\"\n6:   - \"code README\"\n7: ---\n8: \n9: # system skill advisor runtime scripts routing accuracy\n10: \n11: Operator and maintenance scripts for this skill.\n12: \n13: ---\n14: \n15: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n16: \n17: ### Purpose\n18: \n19: This README documents the code-bearing folder `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy` so operators can understand its role without opening every source file first. It follows the sk-doc skill README structure while staying focused on code navigation.\n20: \n21: ### Usage\n22: \n23: Use this file to identify the folder boundary, the likely verification path and the local source files that need sk-code conventions. Keep behavior details in source comments and higher-level workflow details in the owning `SKILL.md`.\n24: \n25: ### Key Statistics\n26: \n27: | Metric | Value |\n28: |---|---:|\n29: | Code files | 14 |\n30: | README scope | Direct files in this folder |\n31: | Audit context | Internal validation notes |\n32: \n33: ---\n34: \n35: ## 2. QUICK START\n36: \n37: **Step 1: Confirm the owner.**\n38: \n39: Start with `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/SKILL.md` for runtime routing and workflow boundaries.\n40: \n41: **Step 2: Inspect the local code.**\n42: \n43: ```bash\n44: rg --files .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy\n45: ```\n46: \n47: Expected result: the command lists the source files summarized below.\n48: \n49: **Step 3: Verify changes.**\n50: \n51: Run individual scripts from the repository root with the documented arguments.\n52: \n53: ---\n54: \n55: ## 3. FEATURES\n56: \n57: | Feature | What It Does |\n58: |---|---|\n59: | Folder boundary | Documents direct code files under `runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy`. |\n60: | sk-code alignment | Points reviewers at OpenCode naming, header, error-handling and type-discipline checks. |\n61: | Verification handoff | Records the expected owner and audit packet for follow-up work. |\n62: \n63: ### Attribution on every run line\n64: \n65: Each line a behaviour run appends carries four fields beside the locator, exit and changed paths:\n66: \n67: - `offeredCommand` is the slash command the advisor head named, or `null` when the head named none.\n68: - `offeredPolicy` is the policy that directly follows that command, one of `run`, `start` or `ask`, or `null` when no policy follows it.\n69: - `offeredAs` says where that command sat in the head: `head`, `partner` when it followed `vs` on an ambiguous head, or `null` when the head named none.\n70: - `briefDelivered` says whether the advisor brief reached the session. It is `true` or `false` where the runtime keeps a readable record, and `unknown` where none exists or cannot be read, so a gap in the record never reads as a miss. A line written when the head moved stops before the offer is read, so it carries none of the four and holds headMoved instead.\n71: \n72: The offer is a replay of the Claude prompt hook after the turn instead of a copy of the live session head. The head is the only text that names both the command and the policy, and not every runtime keeps it, so `replayOffer` runs the checkout's own hook and records what that checkout would have offered at the time of the run. A replay that finds no hook, a failing hook or a head without an offer writes null fields plus an `offerError` of `hook_missing`, `hook_failed` or `no_head`, so one bad line never aborts a run.\n73: \n74: ### Backfill a finished log\n75: \n76: `run-command-behaviour.mjs --backfill <run-log> --out <new-log>` adds those four fields to a log written before they existed, and a `--backfill` run without `--out` is a usage error that exits 2. The mode refuses with exit 2 and a JSON body holding its `code`:\n77: \n78: - `same_path` when `--out` resolves to the input log, because the backfill writes a new file.\n79: - `holdout_row` or `unknown_row` when the corpus refuses a row id, because every prompt is resolved before the first line is written and a refused row leaves no output file behind.\n80: \n81: A line whose locator names no checkout keeps null offer fields and `offerError` `no_checkout`, and the rest of the log is still written with `offerBackfilled: true`.\n82: \n83: A line whose checkout is neither this project root nor an arena holding the `skilled-behaviour-arena` marker also keeps null offer fields, with `offerError` `untrusted_checkout`, because a run log is input and the folder it names may hold any code. Nothing from that folder runs, and the rest of the log is still written.\n84: \n85: `--brief-only` keeps every offer field exactly as the input line holds it, present or absent, and recomputes only `briefDelivered`, because a recorded offer belongs to the head the turn saw and replaying it would describe a different head. Brief delivery now comes from the session records of Pi, Devin, DSH and Codex, while OpenCode stays `unknown` because its plugin adds the brief in memory and the store keeps no copy.\n86: \n87: ### The typed attachment block\n88: \n89: `capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs` reports typed reach twice under `attachedMetrics`:\n90: \n91: - `attachedMetrics.typed` counts `own`, `attached`, `combined` and `total` across the typed rows. `own` is a row the ranked bridge already landed, `attached` is a row the typed command itself delivered, and `combined` is their sum.\n92: - `attachedMetrics.typedRows` keys one record per typed row id in sorted order with `expected_command`, `top`, `carried`, `command` and `policy`. `carried` is `own`, `attached` or null, so a missed row names the command it did carry instead of only lowering a count.\n93: \n94: A typed command travels on its owner. It is delivered only when the leading recommendation is that command's owner skill or carries the same command row, so an unrelated leading skill never presents it. A typed command with no scorer row of its own has no other path to the reader, so its owner is ranked first.\n95: \n96: ### Measure the brief cost\n97: \n98: `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/measure-brief-cost.mjs` reports what the Claude prompt hook actually sends the model for a typed row.\n99: \n100: - Flags: `node measure-brief-cost.mjs [--rows <id,id>] [--root <dir>]` keeps every typed row on the `none` split, `--rows` narrows the run to named corpus ids and `--root` names the checkout the corpus and hook are read from, defaulting to the current directory. A holdout or unknown id exits 2 before anything is spawned, and no prompt is ever printed.\n101: - Output keys: one JSON object holds `rows`, then `liveRows`, `totalBytes`, `medianBytes`, `maxBytes`, `medianHeadBytes` and `maxHeadBytes`. Each entry of `rows` carries `id`, `bytes`, `headBytes`, `estimatedTokens` and `live`, where bytes are the UTF-8 length of `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext`, headBytes is the UTF-8 length of its first line, live says whether that first line starts `Advisor: live`, and tokens divide the context bytes by four, the renderer's own characters per token. Every byte statistic is taken over live rows only.\n102: - Refusal: a run with no live head prints `{\"error\":\"degraded_brief\",...}` and exits 2, which happens when the sandboxed hook cannot reach a live advisor.\n103: - Sandbox: the run makes one `ssa-cost-` directory under the OS temp dir and sets `SPECKIT_IPC_SOCKET_DIR` to it for every hook process, so the operator's warm daemon is never reached. Each hook process also runs without `AI_SESSION_CHILD`, so a run from a dispatched worker measures the brief an operator's own session receives rather than the child form. Each hook child runs with `TMPDIR` set to the sandbox, so a cost run writes no health record into the shared metrics folder. At the end it stops only a process whose command line names that directory and removes it.\n104: \n105: ### Two arms on one commit\n106: \n107: `run-command-behaviour.mjs --arm off` and `--arm on` measure one commit twice. A run under either arm starts from an environment where the advisor switch comes from the arm and not from the operator's shell, so the turn environment differs only in the advisor switch. `--run <n>` takes a whole number from 1 and records it on every line of that run, so two samples of one row stay apart and a retried line still replaces the first line of its own run. After each turn the runner also puts back a `CLAUDE.md` that the turn changed and git does not track, and marks that line `pointerRestored`.\n108: \n109: `--set fresh` reads `behaviour-fresh-prompts.jsonl` beside the script, a file the capture never publishes. The set refuses an absent file with `fresh_missing`, a row whose split is not `fresh` with `fresh_split`, an id that does not start `fresh-` with `fresh_id`, a file that does not hold 36 rows with `set_short`, and a prompt that repeats another row once case and whitespace are folded with `repeated_row`. A fresh row runs only under arm `off` or `on` (`fresh_arm`) and only with `--run` (`fresh_run`).\n110: \n111: `prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs` leaves a `CLAUDE.md` holding `@AGENTS.md` in an arena that has none and appends `/CLAUDE.md` to `.git/info/exclude`, so Claude Code imports the arena's own rules while the clean-tree check and the head stay as they were.\n112: \n113: A summary pairs the two arms row by row. `--pair off,on` gives a runtime a `paired` block when both arms ran and either one scored an owned row, and a row whose arms scored a different number of runs, including a row one arm never scored, adds to `unpaired` and to no other count. `pooled` sums those blocks over every runtime and sets `p` to the exact one-sided sign test over `gained` and `lost`, rounded to four decimals and null when no pair moved either way. A reader that walks every top-level key of the summary must skip `pooled`, because it is not a runtime.\n114: \n115: Each arm prints `declineStarts` and `skillOnlyStarts` as `x of y`, plus one line per entry of `commandStartRuns`. A decline or skill-only run started a tracked command when a skill call named it, a `COMMAND:` line reported it `started`, the host expanded it, or the run read the command's document and also wrote inside its checkout. A read with no write is a lookup and not a start, and a search, an in-place `sed` or a heredoc line is not a read.\n116: \n117: ### Reading the outcome\n118: \n119: - A loaded document is not a followed step, so `loadRate` and `followedRange` say a workflow opened, not that its steps were taken.\n120: - The behaviour rows are tuned rows, so `followedOffered` and `followedNotOffered` describe the corpus the brief was built against, not a fresh one.\n121: - Each row ran once, so one run moves a rate by more than the range around it can settle.\n122: - Two arms differ in more than the brief, so a `--pair` gain or loss is not the brief's effect alone.\n123: - The offer on a stored run of a runtime other than Claude is the Claude hook's replay, recorded when the run was made, so the split by offer says what the Claude head carried for that row.\n124: \n125: ---\n126: \n127: ## 4. STRUCTURE\n128: \n129: | Path | Purpose |\n130: |---|---|\n131: | `gate3-corpus-runner.mjs` | MJS source file in this folder. |\n132: | `score-routing-corpus.py` | PY source file in this folder. |\n133: | `command-prompts.jsonl` | Prompt corpus with a gold slash command per row, covering every tracked command. |\n134: | `capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs` | Scores `command-prompts.jsonl` under the pinned regime and prints or writes the baseline. Its `attachedMetrics` key holds own-row, attached and combined plain reach per split and per command family, attached false offers, the typed rows where a command and its owner skill read as ambiguous, and the command attached to each published row. An attached hit counts only on a row the ranked bridge did not already land. Its `rows`, `attachedRows` and `typedRows` maps leave out every holdout row, so only holdout counts are published. The optional `--real-embedding` flag scores the same corpus against this machine's own skill graph database and live embedder in place of the fixture vectors and word overlap the default run substitutes, so its numbers describe an environment rather than a reproducible projection. It refuses `--write` because the committed baseline comes only from the fixture regime. Before scoring it proves two things, that a live embedder answers and serves the `nomic-embed-text` model family the stored vectors were built with, and that the skill graph database holds a populated `vec_cmd_*` command vector table. A missing piece exits 2 with a message that names it. Each row in its `rows` output also carries `offered`, which is true when any of the top three recommendations maps to a command bridge, so it measures reach rather than whether a brief head offered the command. Its `attachedMetrics.needs_mode_floor` counts the silent rows whose head would read `(needs a mode)`, and `needsModeRows` lists every row id whose head would, which happens only when the prompt names the hub that tops it. `attachedMetrics.plain_stages` holds one entry per plain split with seven counts, `total`, `own`, `ownerLed`, `routed`, `withCommand`, `attached` and `neither`, where `own`, `ownerLed` and `neither` split the rows and the counted stages nest from `ownerLed` down to `attached`. `attachedMetrics.fit` holds the two fit classes, `skill... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n135: | `score-command-transcripts.mjs` | Scores captured sessions through the shared session readers. `--summary <run-log> [<run-log>...]` scores every row one or more run logs list, per runtime and arm, and `--runtime <name> --locator '<json>'` scores the one session a locator names. Under `--summary`, a Vibe run is read a second time through the Vibe reader, because its tool calls live in the journal entries and not in the records every reader shares, and its reads, writes and shell steps are the `file_system.read_file`, `file_system.write_file`, `file_system.edit` and `file_system.bash` calls its journal records. `--row-id <id>` narrows `--summary` to one row and is a usage error with `--runtime`. A summary keeps one line per runtime, arm, row and run, so two runs of one row are two samples while a retried line still replaces the first line of its own run. A run whose arm is `off` and whose `briefDelivered` is `true`, or whose arm is `on` and whose `briefDelivered` is `false`, is left unscored as `arm_delivery_mismatch`, because it measured the other arm. `--json` prints machine-readable output. `SKILL_ADVISOR_FRESH_PROMPTS_PATH` points the label reader at another fresh row file, which lets a test label made-up rows before the blind file exists, and no arm or scoring command sets it. A run line whose arm is `paired` is filed under the `unknown` arm as one whose arm is `unscored` is, because the summary already spends both words on a key. `--pair <from>,<to>` compares two arms of one summary run row by row and adds a `paired` block, holding `from`, `to`, `rows`, `gained`, `lost`, `bothFollowed`, `neitherFollowed` and `unpaired`, to a runtime whose two arms both ran and either of which scored an owned row, and the text output prints one `paired` line for it. `--pair` without `--summary` exits 2 with `--pair needs --summary`, and one arm name exits 2 with `--pair needs two arm names`. A row whose arms scored a different number of runs, including a row one arm never score... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n136: | `command-offer.mjs` | Recovers the attribution fields for a run line. `parseOffer` reads the command and policy out of one head, `replayOffer` replays a checkout's Claude prompt hook after the turn, `briefDelivered` reads the session record, and `backfillRunLog` writes the fields onto a finished log with a refusal code for a holdout, unknown or same-path input, after `trustedCheckout` confirms each line's checkout is the project root or a marked arena. Each offer carries `offeredAs`, `head` or `partner`. A fallback head such as `Advisor: no skill matched.` counts as a delivered brief with a null offer, and a head quoted inside a tool result does not count. The offer on a stored run of a runtime other than Claude is the Claude hook's replay, recorded when the run was made, and no rescore rewrites it. |\n137: | `run-command-behaviour.mjs` | Runs one live turn per row and records the locator, exit and changed paths. `--print-argv <runtime>` prints a runtime's argv, `--list <set>` prints a set's row ids, and a run takes `--runtime` with `--set` or repeated `--row-id`, `--out <run-log>`, optional `--arm`, `--fixture` and `--dry-run`. Without `--arm`, a selection whose ids are all started-command rows runs under arm `g2`, one that holds none runs under `pre` and a mix is refused with `mixed_row_arms`. The `off` and `on` arms compare one commit with the advisor switched off and on, where a turn under either drops every inherited name that ends `_DISABLED` and holds `SKILL_ADVISOR` or `HOOKS`, and the `off` arm then sets `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED` and `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_PLUGIN_DISABLED` to `1`. `--run <n>` takes a whole number from 1 and records it on every line of that run, so two samples of one row stay apart. The `fresh` set reads `behaviour-fresh-prompts.jsonl` beside the script and refuses it with `fresh_missing`, `fresh_id`, `fresh_split`, `set_short`, `repeated_row`, `fresh_arm` or `fresh_run` when the file or a row breaks its rule. Each `g2` run line records the `fixture` folder its prompt named, and `--backfill` replays that folder, or `g2-fixture` beside the log for a line that holds none. `--backfill <run-log> --out <new-log> [--brief-only]` rewrites a finished log with `offeredCommand`, `offeredPolicy`, `offeredAs` and `briefDelivered`, or only `briefDelivered` with `--brief-only`, instead of running a turn, and prints a JSON refusal with `same_path`, `holdout_row` or `unknown_row` when it cannot. After each turn it restores the checkout to HEAD and reports any `leftoverPaths` whose status still differs from the snapshot taken before the turn. Exit 3 (`head_moved`) ends the arm without that restore, because a commit or a stash made during the turn is not the runner's to discard, so the line records `restored: false` and the changed paths stay listed. A... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n138: | `command-routing-baseline.json` | Captured command routing numbers that `tests/command-routing-slice.vitest.ts` holds the scorer to. |\n139: | `build-holdout.mjs` | Builds the frozen holdout from three separately authored fixtures that are disjoint from the labeled corpus, copying each row's own label and keeping its provenance in `source_type` and `origin_fixture`. Family-normalized dedup keeps the first occurrence and drops any prompt that collides with the training corpus. `--write` rewrites `holdout-prompts.jsonl`. |\n140: | `capture-local-native-divergence-ledger.mjs` | Recomputes the strict Python/native top-1 divergence set the ratchet replays. An existing approval is kept only while its prompt hash and both current tops still match, so the output is a capture of live scoring rather than a hand-edited fixture. Scores both scorers against a skill graph indexed fresh into a temporary directory with the clock pinned to the capture day, and `--write` records the result. |\n141: | `capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs` | Captures scorer accuracy across the full corpus, the independent holdout, the frozen ambiguity slice and the named intent buckets under the reproducible filesystem projection, then `--write` records `scorer-eval-baseline.json`. Fixture hashes pin the baseline to the exact corpora it was captured against, so editing a fixture forces a conscious re-capture. `--misses` prints one failing prompt per line. |\n142: | `derive-ambiguity-slice.mjs` | Derives the frozen ambiguity slice as the corpus rows whose top-2 fused-score margin is at or below `tau`, keeping each row's existing gold label, so a regression that only hurts contested prompts still fails a gate. `--write` rewrites `ambiguity-prompts.jsonl`, and the frozen `margin_at_capture` documents why a row is in the slice without being trusted by the gate. |\n143: | `prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs` | Builds the arena a live arm runs in: a clone of the branch tip with no remote and no outer repository, plus the ignored build trees copied in and stamped at copy time so the freshness guard reads them as fresh. It refuses a destination inside a repository or one that already exists, and skips every file whose name begins with `.env` so no secret follows the build output in. When the clone holds no `CLAUDE.md`, the builder writes one holding `@AGENTS.md` and appends `/CLAUDE.md` to `.git/info/exclude`, so Claude Code imports the arena's own rules while the clean-tree check and the head stay as they were. |\n144: | `reject-unknown-options.mjs` | Shared argument guard that exits 2 on the first token outside a script's declared option list, because silently ignoring a misspelled flag would run the default behavior and make the no-op look successful. |\n145: | `score-outcome-rerank.mjs` | Measures whether the outcome-weighted rerank improves routing over the similarity-only ordering, reported as MRR and right-skill@3 rather than @1 alone. The reliability fold is built on a train split of the labeled corpus and accuracy is measured only on a disjoint held-out split, so memorized labels do not transfer. Read-only against the skill graph. |\n146: | `worktree-restore.mjs` | Restores one measured turn's checkout to the snapshot taken before it, leaving a path that was already dirty alone and dropping a staged path HEAD does not know. A second snapshot after the restore reports what still differs, so the caller fails the run instead of claiming a clean worktree. |\n147: | `ambiguity-prompts.jsonl` | Frozen ambiguity slice written by `derive-ambiguity-slice.mjs` and read by `capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs`. Each row carries `id`, `prompt`, `skill_top_1`, `bucket`, `source_type`, `tau`, `margin_at_capture` and `captured_at`. |\n148: | `doctor-prompts.jsonl` | Doctor routing slice read by `tests/doctor-routing-slice.vitest.ts`. Each row carries `id`, `prompt`, `expected_top`, `expected_second` and `wave`, where a wave scores only after every doctor bridge its rows expect is active, so a half-shipped bridge cannot fail the gate. |\n149: | `holdout-prompts.jsonl` | Frozen holdout first written by `build-holdout.mjs` from three separately authored fixtures disjoint from the labeled corpus, and read by `capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs` as its unseen split. The file has been edited by hand since it was sealed, so `build-holdout.mjs --write` would now write a different set of rows. A sealed row is retired by deleting its line and recapturing `scorer-eval-baseline.json`, whose `holdoutSha256` fails the ratchet until that recapture runs. |\n150: | `labeled-prompts.jsonl` | Labeled training corpus with `id`, `prompt`, `skill_top_1`, `skill_correct`, `bucket`, `source_type`, `notes`, `gate3_triggers` and `gate3_reason_category`. Scored by `score-routing-corpus.py`, `score-outcome-rerank.mjs` and `capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs`, and read by the divergence ledger, the ambiguity deriver, the holdout builder and the gate 3 corpus runner. |\n151: | `scorer-eval-baseline.json` | Scorer eval baseline written by `capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs`: `schemaVersion`, `capturedAt`, `capturedAtSha`, `env`, the fixture hashes and `metrics` for the full corpus, holdout, ambiguity slice, gold-none false fire, intent buckets and unknown count. The ratchet gate re-scores live under the same environment and holds every metric to it. |\n152: \n153: ---\n154: \n155: ## 5. CONFIGURATION\n156: \n157: | Setting | Default | Purpose |\n158: |---|---|---|\n159: | sk-code surface | OPENCODE | Applies OpenCode TypeScript, JavaScript, Python, Shell and config conventions. |\n160: | README scope | Direct folder | This file documents this folder, not sibling folders. |\n161: \n162: ---\n163: \n164: ## 6. USAGE EXAMPLES\n165: \n166: **Audit this folder**\n167: \n168: ```text\n169: User request: Check .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy for sk-code and README coverage.\n170: Skill routing: sk-code plus sk-doc.\n171: Expected output: Findings recorded in the 026 audit report.\n172: ```\n173: \n174: **Score captured runs**\n175: \n176: ```bash\n177: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs \\\n178:   --summary scratch/runs-pre.jsonl scratch/runs-post.jsonl --json\n179: ```\n180: \n181: Exit 2 means no score was produced. A missing or malformed log prints `run_log_missing` or `run_log_malformed`, and a session the reader cannot open prints its own code, such as `capability_gated`.\n182: \n183: **Inspect and run one behaviour turn**\n184: \n185: ```bash\n186: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs --print-argv claude\n187: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs \\\n188:   --runtime claude --set chat --out scratch/runs-pre.jsonl --dry-run\n189: ```\n190: \n191: Exit 2 means the run was refused before or during its first prompt: bad flags, an unknown runtime or set, a holdout row, a dirty worktree outside the run log's folder, or a checkout that still has a git remote.\n192: \n193: **Backfill a finished log**\n194: \n195: ```bash\n196: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs \\\n197:   --backfill scratch/runs-pre.jsonl --out scratch/runs-pre-attributed.jsonl\n198: ```\n199: \n200: The output log holds the same lines plus `offeredCommand`, `offeredPolicy`, `offeredAs`, `offerError`, `briefDelivered` and `offerBackfilled`. Exit 2 means the output was refused, and the printed JSON names why: `same_path`, `holdout_row`, `unknown_row` or a missing `--out`.\n201: \n202: ---\n203: \n204: ## 7. TROUBLESHOOTING\n205: \n206: | What You See | Cause | Fix |\n207: |---|---|---|\n208: | README appears stale | Source files changed after this audit | Refresh the structure table and rerun the 026 audit check. |\n209: | Verification command is unclear | Folder is a helper boundary | Use the nearest package or skill-level verification command. |\n210: \n211: ---\n212: \n213: ## 8. RELATED DOCUMENTS\n214: \n215: | Document | Purpose |\n216: |---|---|\n217: | [`system-skill-advisor/SKILL.md`](../../../SKILL.md) | Runtime instructions for the owning skill. |\n218: | [`sk-code/SKILL.md`](../../../../sk-code/SKILL.md) | OpenCode coding standards and verification routing. |\n219: | [`sk-doc skill-readme-template.md`](../../../../sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-readme-template.md) | README structure used for this code README. |"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794760867276,"end":1794760867620}}}}
