{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794760900800,"sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd9ffeEQ4EaGVDFSUKgQ","part":{"partID":"prt_1e015fec40014WfvhnbAno7VqH","sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd9ffeEQ4EaGVDFSUKgQ","messageID":"msg_1e015ed78001PYRFcyFeWihjSv","type":"tool","id":"call_6dd1c50a1bda459b903539a2","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"sed -n '95,270p' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs"},"output":"  cursor: 'its session reader is gated, so no produced transcript can be scored',\n  vibe: 'no live turn is wired for it: live runs need an arena with credentials off and the behaviour arms are stopped',\n});\n\nfunction has(object, key) {\n  return Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(object, key);\n}\n\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// Runtime argv table\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nconst RUNTIMES = {\n  claude: {\n    command: 'claude',\n    model: CLAUDE_MODEL,\n    promptOnStdin: false,\n    // stream-json writes one JSON object per line and every one of them carries\n    // the session id the transcript is stored under.\n    locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'session_id' },\n    // The reader opens a transcript by id plus its own path, and Claude Code names\n    // the file after the session inside a folder per working directory.\n    findTranscript: claudeTranscriptPath,\n    buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n      '-p',\n      prompt,\n      '--model',\n      CLAUDE_MODEL,\n      // stream-json is the only output format that carries the session id the\n      // reader needs after the turn ends.\n      '--output-format',\n      'stream-json',\n      '--verbose',\n      // Print mode has nobody to answer an approval prompt, and the default mode\n      // both denies writes and refuses shell commands, which is exactly the\n      // behaviour an ask-row test must be free to observe.\n      '--permission-mode',\n      'bypassPermissions',\n    ],\n  },\n  codex: {\n    command: 'codex',\n    model: CODEX_MODEL,\n    promptOnStdin: true,\n    // The run opens with an event naming its thread, and the rollout file is\n    // named after that thread id.\n    locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'thread_id' },\n    // The rollout file is named after the thread id the stream reported, so the id\n    // alone leads back to it once the store has been searched.\n    findTranscript: codexTranscriptPath,\n    buildArgv: () => [\n      'exec',\n      // The JSON event stream is how a turn reports its thread id.\n      '--json',\n      '--model',\n      CODEX_MODEL,\n      '-c',\n      'model_reasoning_effort=medium',\n      '-c',\n      'service_tier=fast',\n      // A headless run cannot answer an approval prompt, and `exec` defaults to a\n      // read-only sandbox that would silently swallow every write.\n      '-c',\n      'approval_policy=never',\n      '--sandbox',\n      'workspace-write',\n      '-',\n    ],\n  },\n  opencode: {\n    command: 'opencode',\n    model: OPENCODE_MODEL,\n    promptOnStdin: false,\n    // The turn's own events carry the id of the session it created.\n    locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'sessionID' },\n    buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n      // `opencode run` takes no --dir, and the child already starts in the\n      // checkout, so passing the directory a second time would only fail.\n      'run',\n      // Without this the turn is handed to the operator's long-running background\n      // service, whose tools run with that service's environment. The hardened\n      // environment this runner builds would then never reach a tool call.\n      '--standalone',\n      '--model',\n      OPENCODE_MODEL,\n      '--format',\n      'json',\n      // Auto-approve everything not explicitly denied. A run that stalls on a\n      // permission prompt reads as a model that declined to act.\n      '--auto',\n      prompt,\n    ],\n  },\n  pi: {\n    command: 'pi',\n    model: PI_MODEL,\n    promptOnStdin: false,\n    // Print mode never reports the session it wrote, so the runner mints the id\n    // and passes it on the command line instead of recovering it from the stream.\n    locator: { kind: 'runner' },\n    buildArgv: ({ prompt, sessionId }) => [\n      '-p',\n      // Without it a non-interactive Pi probes the network on startup and can\n      // hang before it ever reads the prompt.\n      '--offline',\n      '--model',\n      PI_MODEL,\n      // Without a minted id the log would have to be guessed from whatever\n      // file is newest, which can open a neighbouring turn's transcript.\n      '--session-id',\n      sessionId,\n      prompt,\n    ],\n  },\n  devin: {\n    command: 'devin',\n    model: DEVIN_MODEL,\n    promptOnStdin: false,\n    // A Devin turn keeps its session in a store its own output does not name, so\n    // the id comes from the listing of the directory the turn ran in.\n    locator: { kind: 'listing', args: ['list', '--format', 'json'] },\n    buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n      '-p',\n      '--model',\n      DEVIN_MODEL,\n      // Under `accept-edits` every tool call that wants confirmation is refused,\n      // so a headless turn explores its whole budget and writes nothing.\n      '--permission-mode',\n      'dangerous',\n      // A fresh checkout has never been trusted interactively, and print mode has\n      // nobody to answer the trust prompt, so the turn would refuse to start.\n      '--respect-workspace-trust',\n      'false',\n      // No `--sandbox` here. That flag forces a permission mode that ignores\n      // `dangerous` and rejects every write in print mode, so a turn would write\n      // nothing and read as a model that declined. Containment comes from the\n      // arena instead.\n      // The separator keeps a prompt that opens with a dash from parsing as a flag.\n      '--',\n      prompt,\n    ],\n  },\n  dsh: {\n    command: 'dsh',\n    // dsh takes no model flag at all. The roster route patch decides the model,\n    // and this table pins the verified LLM Gateway route.\n    model: DSH_MODEL,\n    promptOnStdin: true,\n    // The harness names the session itself and refuses an id it never created,\n    // so the turn's id is recovered from the store folder its log lands in.\n    locator: { kind: 'store', list: dshSessionIds },\n    buildArgv: () => [\n      '--profile',\n      'headless',\n      '--patch',\n      resolve(DSH_PATCH_DIR, 'route-llmgateway-deepseek.patch.yml'),\n      // No lineage patch: it marks the run as a fan-out leaf, and the repo\n      // guards keep the prompt advisor off for a leaf. A chat turn has to\n      // receive the brief like a person's session would.\n      '--patch',\n      resolve(DSH_PATCH_DIR, 'guards.patch.yml'),\n      '--json',\n      '--permission-mode',\n      'workspace-write',\n      // Approvals default to ask, and a headless turn has nobody to answer.\n      '--approval-policy',\n      'never',\n      // The dash takes the prompt from stdin, which is the channel a headless\n      // run reads, and keeps a prompt that opens with one from parsing as a\n      // flag.\n      '-',\n    ],\n  },\n};\n\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"  cursor: 'its session reader is gated, so no produced transcript can be scored',\n  vibe: 'no live turn is wired for it: live runs need an arena with credentials off and the behaviour arms are stopped',\n});\n\nfunction has(object, key) {\n  return Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(object, key);\n}\n\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// Runtime argv table\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nconst RUNTIMES = {\n  claude: {\n    command: 'claude',\n    model: CLAUDE_MODEL,\n    promptOnStdin: false,\n    // stream-json writes one JSON object per line and every one of them carries\n    // the session id the transcript is stored under.\n    locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'session_id' },\n    // The reader opens a transcript by id plus its own path, and Claude Code names\n    // the file after the session inside a folder per working directory.\n    findTranscript: claudeTranscriptPath,\n    buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n      '-p',\n      prompt,\n      '--model',\n      CLAUDE_MODEL,\n      // stream-json is the only output format that carries the session id the\n      // reader needs after the turn ends.\n      '--output-format',\n      'stream-json',\n      '--verbose',\n      // Print mode has nobody to answer an approval prompt, and the default mode\n      // both denies writes and refuses shell commands, which is exactly the\n      // behaviour an ask-row test must be free to observe.\n      '--permission-mode',\n      'bypassPermissions',\n    ],\n  },\n  codex: {\n    command: 'codex',\n    model: CODEX_MODEL,\n    promptOnStdin: true,\n    // The run opens with an event naming its thread, and the rollout file is\n    // named after that thread id.\n    locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'thread_id' },\n    // The rollout file is named after the thread id the stream reported, so the id\n    // alone leads back to it once the store has been searched.\n    findTranscript: codexTranscriptPath,\n    buildArgv: () => [\n      'exec',\n      // The JSON event stream is how a turn reports its thread id.\n      '--json',\n      '--model',\n      CODEX_MODEL,\n      '-c',\n      'model_reasoning_effort=medium',\n      '-c',\n      'service_tier=fast',\n      // A headless run cannot answer an approval prompt, and `exec` defaults to a\n      // read-only sandbox that would silently swallow every write.\n      '-c',\n      'approval_policy=never',\n      '--sandbox',\n      'workspace-write',\n      '-',\n    ],\n  },\n  opencode: {\n    command: 'opencode',\n    model: OPENCODE_MODEL,\n    promptOnStdin: false,\n    // The turn's own events carry the id of the session it created.\n    locator: { kind: 'stream', field: 'sessionID' },\n    buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n      // `opencode run` takes no --dir, and the child already starts in the\n      // checkout, so passing the directory a second time would only fail.\n      'run',\n      // Without this the turn is handed to the operator's long-running background\n      // service, whose tools run with that service's environment. The hardened\n      // environment this runner builds would then never reach a tool call.\n      '--standalone',\n      '--model',\n      OPENCODE_MODEL,\n      '--format',\n      'json',\n      // Auto-approve everything not explicitly denied. A run that stalls on a\n      // permission prompt reads as a model that declined to act.\n      '--auto',\n      prompt,\n    ],\n  },\n  pi: {\n    command: 'pi',\n    model: PI_MODEL,\n    promptOnStdin: false,\n    // Print mode never reports the session it wrote, so the runner mints the id\n    // and passes it on the command line instead of recovering it from the stream.\n    locator: { kind: 'runner' },\n    buildArgv: ({ prompt, sessionId }) => [\n      '-p',\n      // Without it a non-interactive Pi probes the network on startup and can\n      // hang before it ever reads the prompt.\n      '--offline',\n      '--model',\n      PI_MODEL,\n      // Without a minted id the log would have to be guessed from whatever\n      // file is newest, which can open a neighbouring turn's transcript.\n      '--session-id',\n      sessionId,\n      prompt,\n    ],\n  },\n  devin: {\n    command: 'devin',\n    model: DEVIN_MODEL,\n    promptOnStdin: false,\n    // A Devin turn keeps its session in a store its own output does not name, so\n    // the id comes from the listing of the directory the turn ran in.\n    locator: { kind: 'listing', args: ['list', '--format', 'json'] },\n    buildArgv: ({ prompt }) => [\n      '-p',\n      '--model',\n      DEVIN_MODEL,\n      // Under `accept-edits` every tool call that wants confirmation is refused,\n      // so a headless turn explores its whole budget and writes nothing.\n      '--permission-mode',\n      'dangerous',\n      // A fresh checkout has never been trusted interactively, and print mode has\n      // nobody to answer the trust prompt, so the turn would refuse to start.\n      '--respect-workspace-trust',\n      'false',\n      // No `--sandbox` here. That flag forces a permission mode that ignores\n      // `dangerous` and rejects every write in print mode, so a turn would write\n      // nothing and read as a model that declined. Containment comes from the\n      // arena instead.\n      // The separator keeps a prompt that opens with a dash from parsing as a flag.\n      '--',\n      prompt,\n    ],\n  },\n  dsh: {\n    command: 'dsh',\n    // dsh takes no model flag at all. The roster route patch decides the model,\n    // and this table pins the verified LLM Gateway route.\n    model: DSH_MODEL,\n    promptOnStdin: true,\n    // The harness names the session itself and refuses an id it never created,\n    // so the turn's id is recovered from the store folder its log lands in.\n    locator: { kind: 'store', list: dshSessionIds },\n    buildArgv: () => [\n      '--profile',\n      'headless',\n      '--patch',\n      resolve(DSH_PATCH_DIR, 'route-llmgateway-deepseek.patch.yml'),\n      // No lineage patch: it marks the run as a fan-out leaf, and the repo\n      // guards keep the prompt advisor off for a leaf. A chat turn has to\n      // receive the brief like a person's session would.\n      '--patch',\n      resolve(DSH_PATCH_DIR, 'guards.patch.yml'),\n      '--json',\n      '--permission-mode',\n      'workspace-write',\n      // Approvals default to ask, and a headless turn has nobody to answer.\n      '--approval-policy',\n      'never',\n      // The dash takes the prompt from stdin, which is the channel a headless\n      // run reads, and keeps a prompt that opens with one from parsing as a\n      // flag.\n      '-',\n    ],\n  },\n};\n\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"sed -n '95,270p' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs\"}"},"time":{"start":1794760900292,"end":1794760900800}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794760901688,"sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd9ffeEQ4EaGVDFSUKgQ","part":{"id":"prt_1e0160438001D0ZNcbqpSasgM3","sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd9ffeEQ4EaGVDFSUKgQ","messageID":"msg_1e015ed78001PYRFcyFeWihjSv","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"a88e44b8963b86bfa54f304a61c9626b1ccf272d","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":6203,"output":73,"reasoning":177,"cache":{"read":117120,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794760903359,"sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd9ffeEQ4EaGVDFSUKgQ","part":{"id":"prt_1e0160abf001gaDsL6fpynjfbk","sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd9ffeEQ4EaGVDFSUKgQ","messageID":"msg_1e016043d001K4JCCbDoL2qQ0Z","type":"step-start","snapshot":"a88e44b8963b86bfa54f304a61c9626b1ccf272d"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794760905689,"sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd9ffeEQ4EaGVDFSUKgQ","part":{"partID":"prt_1e01612860011vZcFGic4VjJvk","sessionID":"ses_e1feefdd9ffeEQ4EaGVDFSUKgQ","messageID":"msg_1e016043d001K4JCCbDoL2qQ0Z","type":"tool","id":"call_52774994c12a4ca09f8b8209","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md"},"output":"Read file .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md, lines 1-134\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Command bridges\"\n3: description: \"Authored command-bridge inputs, compatibility evidence and the generated projection consumed by advisor scoring surfaces.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"command bridges\"\n6:   - \"generated command bridge projection\"\n7:   - \"command bridge compatibility\"\n8: ---\n9: \n10: # Command bridges\n11: \n12: ---\n13: \n14: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n15: \n16: `command-bridges/` is the source boundary for command metadata that is not yet represented uniformly by every scoring surface. The deriver combines live skill metadata with the authored compatibility inputs and writes the generated JSON projection.\n17: \n18: The doctor commands are a third source: the deriver reads `.skilled/commands/doctor/_routes.yaml` and writes one bridge per doctor route and per standalone doctor command and per MCP sub-route.\n19: \n20: Each hub's `mode-registry.json` is a fourth source: the deriver reads the `command` of every mode and writes the `MODE_COMMANDS` table the advisor uses to attach a slash command to a hub recommendation.\n21: \n22: ---\n23: \n24: ## 2. CONTENTS\n25: \n26: | File | Responsibility | Authority |\n27: |---|---|---|\n28: | `command-bridges.generated.json` | Stores the derived command-bridge projection. | Generated, do not edit |\n29: | `derive-command-bridges.cjs` | Reads live metadata and authored inputs, validates uniqueness and writes the projection. | Authored deriver |\n30: | `scoring-compatibility.json` | Holds TypeScript and Python scoring compatibility mappings. | Authored input |\n31: | `shadow-diff.md` | Records comparison evidence between generated and live bridge authorities. | Authored evidence |\n32: \n33: ---\n34: \n35: ## 3. DERIVATION FLOW\n36: \n37: ```text\n38: skill command metadata files\n39: scoring-compatibility.json\n40: .skilled/commands/doctor/_routes.yaml\n41: hub mode-registry.json files\n42:                          |\n43:                          v\n44:               derive-command-bridges.cjs\n45:                          |\n46:                          v\n47:               command-bridges.generated.json\n48: ```\n49: \n50: The deriver also updates generated projection blocks in the live TypeScript and Python scoring sources. Its `GENERATED COMMAND ROWS` block in `lib/command-attach.ts` holds `COMMAND_ROWS`, one row per id a scorer can return for an enabled entry, covering the entry id and every enabled runtime id. Each row carries `command`, the runnable slash command, `policy`, its derived policy, `mutating`, the class that says what a run writes (`read-only`, `add-only` or `mutates`) when the record names one, and `ownerSkillId`, the owning skill. It writes the `MODE_COMMANDS` block into the same file, one row per hub mode that names a command, and fails when a mode names a command with no file under `.skilled/commands/`. The same block also holds `TRACKED_COMMANDS`, one row per tracked command keyed by its runnable string, emitted for every bridge entry whether or not the bridge is active. A surface that attaches a command reads its policy from `TRACKED_COMMANDS`. Each TypeScript bridge in `lib/scorer/projection.ts` carries an `ownerSkill`, the skill that owns its command. The generated JSON and generated source blocks are outputs, not hand-edited inputs.\n51: \n52: Every tracked row carries the command's derived policy, the skill that owns it and `scorerRow`, which is true when the TypeScript scorer has a row for that entry. A typed command therefore always has a policy and an owner to deliver, even where no scorer row exists.\n53: \n54: Each bridge row with TypeScript enabled also carries an `embeddingText`, the plain sentence a scoring vector is built from. A Python-only row carries none. A metadata row leads with its `userIntent.job` sentence and adds every `userIntent.ownedSignals` phrase, because both are written in the asker's voice. The entry `description` stands in for the job only when the record states no job. A doctor row builds it from the router frontmatter `description` and the `trigger_phrases` of the route. The deriver drops each slash command string before the parts are joined, because a name left in would hand the scorer a second copy of the token it already matches literally. The field is derived on every run and never authored, so edit the sources rather than the JSON.\n55: \n56: ---\n57: \n58: ## 4. DOCTOR BRIDGES\n59: \n60: Ids are `command-doctor-<command>-<target>` when the doctor command owns more than one route, and `command-doctor-<command>` otherwise. `skillId` and `ownerMode` come from the router's `skill_agent` tag. A router without the tag fails the deriver with a named error.\n61: \n62: The runnable string is the command plus its target when the command owns more than one route. A standalone entry may set `runnable`, as `/doctor:env` does with `/doctor:env list`.\n63: \n64: A new route needs a hand-written entry in both the `typescript` and `python` lists in `scoring-compatibility.json`. Each entry carries only `inventoryId`, `order`, `id` (equal to `inventoryId`) and an empty `description`.\n65: \n66: The deriver fills the TypeScript name, keywords, intent signals and domains from the manifest, and the Python slash marker from the runnable string. A route marked `default_target` in `_routes.yaml` also gets the bare command (for example `/doctor:ci`) as a TypeScript keyword and a Python slash marker, so the bare typed command reaches it. A typed `<command> <target>` names that target, so the bare command inside it does not also match the default target. Python matches a doctor bridge only on a typed slash command.\n67: \n68: A doctor bridge passes on its own wording (an explicit-author phrase match). When none passes and no `/doctor:` command was typed, the scorer asks `/doctor:helper`'s own ranker, `doctor-helper-match.cjs`, whether the prompt describes a doctor problem. If it does, `command-doctor-helper` passes, and `/doctor:helper` then names the command. With no skill passing, a ranker score of 10 or more is enough, or 6 or more when the prompt names a problem. Beside a passing skill, the prompt must name a wrong state or a health check, such as `is not registered`, `never converges` or `turn off`. A `/doctor:` command counts as typed when the prompt starts with `/doctor:` or when it directly follows `run`, `execute`, `start`, `launch`, `use` or `kick off` outside quotes and backticks. A quoted command or a bare mid-sentence mention does not count. A typed command puts its own passing bridge first. A prompt that contains one of a bridge's `lead_phrases` also puts that passing bridge first, so `check my environment variables` leads with `/doctor:env`. For other prompts, a passing skill keeps first place and passing doctor bridges sit right after the second passing skill or the only passing skill. This keeps a doctor line within the CLI's three shown results. A typed command shows its matching runnable string on the top line. A skill-first brief shows `Also: /doctor:<command> <target> (<policy>)` on the second line. The policy is run, start or ask, or `parent-named only` in a child session for a command the child must not start on its own, and the line is left out when the head already names that command. The Python fallback matches a doctor bridge only on a typed slash command.\n69: \n70: Each `mcp_subroutes` entry becomes the bridge `command-doctor-mcp-<sub_action>`, with the runnable string `/doctor:mcp <sub_action>` and its owner from the `skill_agent` tag in `mcp.md`. Every derived doctor bridge is active in both scorers, and `doctor-phrase-reach.cjs` proves each one.\n71: \n72: Check every admitted phrase with `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/doctor-phrase-reach.cjs`.\n73: \n74: Every command bridge, not only a doctor bridge, passes on its own wording. `requireCommandPhraseAnchor` in `lib/scorer/fusion.ts` drops a passing command row below threshold unless the explicit-author lane matched one of its phrases or its slash string. The one other way in is meaning: a real embedder cosine of at least 0.72 against the row's `embeddingText` vector, with a 0.02 lead over the next command row. That needs the local embedder, so without it every command row passes on its wording alone. A prompt that asks for no change rules out every command on meaning, and a read-only question admits only a bridge marked `readOnly`.\n75: \n76: ---\n77: \n78: ## 5. STANDALONE SKILL COMMANDS\n79: \n80: A standalone skill has a `command-metadata.json` and no `mode-registry.json`, as `sk-prompt` and `system-spec-kit` do. Its commands are active in both scorers by construction and need no row in `scoring-compatibility.json`. The deriver builds each row from the metadata entry:\n81: \n82: - TypeScript: the `userIntent.ownedSignals` phrases become the keywords and intent signals, the command's namespace and `command` become the domains, and a `read-only` `mutating` class sets `readOnly: true`.\n83: - Python: the slash markers are the command with and without its leading slash, and `owningSkill` is the skill.\n84: \n85: A command needs at least two `userIntent.ownedSignals`, or the deriver fails. A hand row in `scoring-compatibility.json` still wins where one exists. Generated rows take orders after the highest hand-written order.\n86: \n87: ---\n88: \n89: ## 6. HUMAN EDIT BOUNDARY\n90: \n91: Humans may edit `derive-command-bridges.cjs`, `scoring-compatibility.json` and `shadow-diff.md`. Regenerate `command-bridges.generated.json` after changing an authored input or the deriver. Do not edit the generated JSON directly.\n92: \n93: ---\n94: \n95: ## 7. VALIDATION\n96: \n97: Run the deriver in check mode from the repository root:\n98: \n99: ```bash\n100: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/derive-command-bridges.cjs --check\n101: ```\n102: \n103: ---\n104: \n105: ## 8. RISK KEYS AND COMMAND POLICY\n106: \n107: The command tree `.skilled/commands/<namespace>/<name>.md` owns which commands exist. The deriver fails when a command file has no `command-metadata.json` entry and no doctor route. It fails again when a declaration names a command with no file.\n108: \n109: A `command-metadata.json` entry and a doctor route or action carry the same risk keys. A value outside the accepted set fails the deriver before any policy is derived.\n110: \n111: | Risk key | Accepted values | Applies to |\n112: |---|---|---|\n113: | `mutating` | `read-only`, `add-only`, `mutates` | Every metadata entry and every doctor route or action |\n114: | `gate3_location` | A non-empty string naming what the command writes | Required on every metadata entry |\n115: | `ask_first` | `settings`, `outside-repo`, `spend`, `relaunch` | Optional on both records |\n116: | `write_scope` | `spec-folder` | Optional on both records |\n117: \n118: `derivePolicy` reads those keys in order and stops at the first match.\n119: \n120: | Step | Condition | Policy |\n121: |---|---|---|\n122: | 1 | `ask_first` lists at least one reason | `ask` |\n123: | 2 | A doctor route or action classed `mutates` | `ask` |\n124: | 3 | `mutating` is `read-only` | `run` |\n125: | 4 | `mutating` is `add-only` with `write_scope` set to `spec-folder` | `run` |\n126: | 5 | Any other record | `start` |\n127: \n128: `commandPolicies` in `command-bridges.generated.json` holds one record per command, or one record per route or action for a doctor command. Each record carries five keys: `command`, `route`, `mutating`, `askFirst`, `policy`. Nothing scores on it yet.\n129: \n130: ---\n131: \n132: ## 9. RELATED\n133: \n134: - [`Skill-advisor scripts`](../README.md)","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md, lines 1-134\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Command bridges\"\n3: description: \"Authored command-bridge inputs, compatibility evidence and the generated projection consumed by advisor scoring surfaces.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"command bridges\"\n6:   - \"generated command bridge projection\"\n7:   - \"command bridge compatibility\"\n8: ---\n9: \n10: # Command bridges\n11: \n12: ---\n13: \n14: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n15: \n16: `command-bridges/` is the source boundary for command metadata that is not yet represented uniformly by every scoring surface. The deriver combines live skill metadata with the authored compatibility inputs and writes the generated JSON projection.\n17: \n18: The doctor commands are a third source: the deriver reads `.skilled/commands/doctor/_routes.yaml` and writes one bridge per doctor route and per standalone doctor command and per MCP sub-route.\n19: \n20: Each hub's `mode-registry.json` is a fourth source: the deriver reads the `command` of every mode and writes the `MODE_COMMANDS` table the advisor uses to attach a slash command to a hub recommendation.\n21: \n22: ---\n23: \n24: ## 2. CONTENTS\n25: \n26: | File | Responsibility | Authority |\n27: |---|---|---|\n28: | `command-bridges.generated.json` | Stores the derived command-bridge projection. | Generated, do not edit |\n29: | `derive-command-bridges.cjs` | Reads live metadata and authored inputs, validates uniqueness and writes the projection. | Authored deriver |\n30: | `scoring-compatibility.json` | Holds TypeScript and Python scoring compatibility mappings. | Authored input |\n31: | `shadow-diff.md` | Records comparison evidence between generated and live bridge authorities. | Authored evidence |\n32: \n33: ---\n34: \n35: ## 3. DERIVATION FLOW\n36: \n37: ```text\n38: skill command metadata files\n39: scoring-compatibility.json\n40: .skilled/commands/doctor/_routes.yaml\n41: hub mode-registry.json files\n42:                          |\n43:                          v\n44:               derive-command-bridges.cjs\n45:                          |\n46:                          v\n47:               command-bridges.generated.json\n48: ```\n49: \n50: The deriver also updates generated projection blocks in the live TypeScript and Python scoring sources. Its `GENERATED COMMAND ROWS` block in `lib/command-attach.ts` holds `COMMAND_ROWS`, one row per id a scorer can return for an enabled entry, covering the entry id and every enabled runtime id. Each row carries `command`, the runnable slash command, `policy`, its derived policy, `mutating`, the class that says what a run writes (`read-only`, `add-only` or `mutates`) when the record names one, and `ownerSkillId`, the owning skill. It writes the `MODE_COMMANDS` block into the same file, one row per hub mode that names a command, and fails when a mode names a command with no file under `.skilled/commands/`. The same block also holds `TRACKED_COMMANDS`, one row per tracked command keyed by its runnable string, emitted for every bridge entry whether or not the bridge is active. A surface that attaches a command reads its policy from `TRACKED_COMMANDS`. Each TypeScript bridge in `lib/scorer/projection.ts` carries an `ownerSkill`, the skill that owns its command. The generated JSON and generated source blocks are outputs, not hand-edited inputs.\n51: \n52: Every tracked row carries the command's derived policy, the skill that owns it and `scorerRow`, which is true when the TypeScript scorer has a row for that entry. A typed command therefore always has a policy and an owner to deliver, even where no scorer row exists.\n53: \n54: Each bridge row with TypeScript enabled also carries an `embeddingText`, the plain sentence a scoring vector is built from. A Python-only row carries none. A metadata row leads with its `userIntent.job` sentence and adds every `userIntent.ownedSignals` phrase, because both are written in the asker's voice. The entry `description` stands in for the job only when the record states no job. A doctor row builds it from the router frontmatter `description` and the `trigger_phrases` of the route. The deriver drops each slash command string before the parts are joined, because a name left in would hand the scorer a second copy of the token it already matches literally. The field is derived on every run and never authored, so edit the sources rather than the JSON.\n55: \n56: ---\n57: \n58: ## 4. DOCTOR BRIDGES\n59: \n60: Ids are `command-doctor-<command>-<target>` when the doctor command owns more than one route, and `command-doctor-<command>` otherwise. `skillId` and `ownerMode` come from the router's `skill_agent` tag. A router without the tag fails the deriver with a named error.\n61: \n62: The runnable string is the command plus its target when the command owns more than one route. A standalone entry may set `runnable`, as `/doctor:env` does with `/doctor:env list`.\n63: \n64: A new route needs a hand-written entry in both the `typescript` and `python` lists in `scoring-compatibility.json`. Each entry carries only `inventoryId`, `order`, `id` (equal to `inventoryId`) and an empty `description`.\n65: \n66: The deriver fills the TypeScript name, keywords, intent signals and domains from the manifest, and the Python slash marker from the runnable string. A route marked `default_target` in `_routes.yaml` also gets the bare command (for example `/doctor:ci`) as a TypeScript keyword and a Python slash marker, so the bare typed command reaches it. A typed `<command> <target>` names that target, so the bare command inside it does not also match the default target. Python matches a doctor bridge only on a typed slash command.\n67: \n68: A doctor bridge passes on its own wording (an explicit-author phrase match). When none passes and no `/doctor:` command was typed, the scorer asks `/doctor:helper`'s own ranker, `doctor-helper-match.cjs`, whether the prompt describes a doctor problem. If it does, `command-doctor-helper` passes, and `/doctor:helper` then names the command. With no skill passing, a ranker score of 10 or more is enough, or 6 or more when the prompt names a problem. Beside a passing skill, the prompt must name a wrong state or a health check, such as `is not registered`, `never converges` or `turn off`. A `/doctor:` command counts as typed when the prompt starts with `/doctor:` or when it directly follows `run`, `execute`, `start`, `launch`, `use` or `kick off` outside quotes and backticks. A quoted command or a bare mid-sentence mention does not count. A typed command puts its own passing bridge first. A prompt that contains one of a bridge's `lead_phrases` also puts that passing bridge first, so `check my environment variables` leads with `/doctor:env`. For other prompts, a passing skill keeps first place and passing doctor bridges sit right after the second passing skill or the only passing skill. This keeps a doctor line within the CLI's three shown results. A typed command shows its matching runnable string on the top line. A skill-first brief shows `Also: /doctor:<command> <target> (<policy>)` on the second line. The policy is run, start or ask, or `parent-named only` in a child session for a command the child must not start on its own, and the line is left out when the head already names that command. The Python fallback matches a doctor bridge only on a typed slash command.\n69: \n70: Each `mcp_subroutes` entry becomes the bridge `command-doctor-mcp-<sub_action>`, with the runnable string `/doctor:mcp <sub_action>` and its owner from the `skill_agent` tag in `mcp.md`. Every derived doctor bridge is active in both scorers, and `doctor-phrase-reach.cjs` proves each one.\n71: \n72: Check every admitted phrase with `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/doctor-phrase-reach.cjs`.\n73: \n74: Every command bridge, not only a doctor bridge, passes on its own wording. `requireCommandPhraseAnchor` in `lib/scorer/fusion.ts` drops a passing command row below threshold unless the explicit-author lane matched one of its phrases or its slash string. The one other way in is meaning: a real embedder cosine of at least 0.72 against the row's `embeddingText` vector, with a 0.02 lead over the next command row. That needs the local embedder, so without it every command row passes on its wording alone. A prompt that asks for no change rules out every command on meaning, and a read-only question admits only a bridge marked `readOnly`.\n75: \n76: ---\n77: \n78: ## 5. STANDALONE SKILL COMMANDS\n79: \n80: A standalone skill has a `command-metadata.json` and no `mode-registry.json`, as `sk-prompt` and `system-spec-kit` do. Its commands are active in both scorers by construction and need no row in `scoring-compatibility.json`. The deriver builds each row from the metadata entry:\n81: \n82: - TypeScript: the `userIntent.ownedSignals` phrases become the keywords and intent signals, the command's namespace and `command` become the domains, and a `read-only` `mutating` class sets `readOnly: true`.\n83: - Python: the slash markers are the command with and without its leading slash, and `owningSkill` is the skill.\n84: \n85: A command needs at least two `userIntent.ownedSignals`, or the deriver fails. A hand row in `scoring-compatibility.json` still wins where one exists. Generated rows take orders after the highest hand-written order.\n86: \n87: ---\n88: \n89: ## 6. HUMAN EDIT BOUNDARY\n90: \n91: Humans may edit `derive-command-bridges.cjs`, `scoring-compatibility.json` and `shadow-diff.md`. Regenerate `command-bridges.generated.json` after changing an authored input or the deriver. Do not edit the generated JSON directly.\n92: \n93: ---\n94: \n95: ## 7. VALIDATION\n96: \n97: Run the deriver in check mode from the repository root:\n98: \n99: ```bash\n100: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/derive-command-bridges.cjs --check\n101: ```\n102: \n103: ---\n104: \n105: ## 8. RISK KEYS AND COMMAND POLICY\n106: \n107: The command tree `.skilled/commands/<namespace>/<name>.md` owns which commands exist. The deriver fails when a command file has no `command-metadata.json` entry and no doctor route. It fails again when a declaration names a command with no file.\n108: \n109: A `command-metadata.json` entry and a doctor route or action carry the same risk keys. A value outside the accepted set fails the deriver before any policy is derived.\n110: \n111: | Risk key | Accepted values | Applies to |\n112: |---|---|---|\n113: | `mutating` | `read-only`, `add-only`, `mutates` | Every metadata entry and every doctor route or action |\n114: | `gate3_location` | A non-empty string naming what the command writes | Required on every metadata entry |\n115: | `ask_first` | `settings`, `outside-repo`, `spend`, `relaunch` | Optional on both records |\n116: | `write_scope` | `spec-folder` | Optional on both records |\n117: \n118: `derivePolicy` reads those keys in order and stops at the first match.\n119: \n120: | Step | Condition | Policy |\n121: |---|---|---|\n122: | 1 | `ask_first` lists at least one reason | `ask` |\n123: | 2 | A doctor route or action classed `mutates` | `ask` |\n124: | 3 | `mutating` is `read-only` | `run` |\n125: | 4 | `mutating` is `add-only` with `write_scope` set to `spec-folder` | `run` |\n126: | 5 | Any other record | `start` |\n127: \n128: `commandPolicies` in `command-bridges.generated.json` holds one record per command, or one record per route or action for a doctor command. Each record carries five keys: `command`, `route`, `mutating`, `askFirst`, `policy`. Nothing scores on it yet.\n129: \n130: ---\n131: \n132: ## 9. RELATED\n133: \n134: - [`Skill-advisor scripts`](../README.md)"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/command-bridges/README.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794760905350,"end":1794760905689}}}}
